From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lei Wang <wangzl@amazon.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 23:14:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] Add a start-up guard that stops injecting into a
 container that keeps failing

When an injected auto-instrumentation agent keeps an application from
starting (for example a dependency bundled with the agent shadowing the
application's own version), Kubernetes restarts the same container in
place and the agent is injected again every time, so the container
crash-loops forever. The guard uses those restarts: at the start of
every process, before any environment variable is modified, it looks at
how the previous runs of the container went and, if they kept failing
with the agent, injects nothing for this run.

Rules (constants):
- 3 consecutive runs that did not stay up for 60 s after the agent
  finished initialising, each start less than 360 s after the previous
  one (a run is a "fast failure"), or 2 consecutive runs that crashed
  on an uncaught exception raised inside agent code -> stop injecting.
- 2 further fast failures while not injecting -> the application fails
  without the agent too; inject again and never stop in this pod.

State: a few small files in the agent volume (the parent of the
injector/ directory holding libotelinject.so, taken from LD_PRELOAD),
named after the container (read from the kubelet's termination-log
bind mount in /proc/self/mountinfo, else a hash of /proc/1/cmdline).
The injector writes the counters; the agents report "initialised",
"healthy after 60 s" and "crashed inside the agent". Only the first
process of a container run (a new PID 1 start time) updates the state;
all other processes of that run follow the recorded decision. Every
write is atomic (temporary file + rename), the set of files and their
sizes are bounded.

The guard is active only on Kubernetes, only for the runtimes whose
agent path is configured in the process, and can be turned off with
AWS_INSTRUMENTATION_GUARD_ENABLED=false. Any error makes it behave as
if it did not exist (inject). It uses raw Linux syscalls only, so it
adds no libc dependency and no panic paths.
